    "subtitle": "Skill: joining four tables",
    "briefing": "A chain's orders are split over four tables. orders says who (customer_id), what (product_id) and where (store_id), with qty and a discount_pct. customers has names, products has names and unit_price, stores has each store's name and region.\n\nBuild one table with a row for every order (all 80, none lost, none doubled) and these columns: order_id, customer_name, product_name, store_name, region and line_total.\n\nline_total is what the customer paid: qty times unit_price, reduced by the order's discount_pct percent (a discount_pct of 10 means 10 percent off).\n\nSome orders name a customer or a store that is not in its table. Keep those orders; the missing names and region simply stay empty.\n\nIn Python, df holds the orders, and customers, products and stores hold the rest; put your answer in df (df = ...). That table is what gets judged.\n\nIn SQL, data holds the orders and customers, products and stores the rest. Create a NEW table named result that holds your answer: CREATE TABLE result AS SELECT ...; That table is what gets judged. You can run your query as often as you like; each run replaces the old result.",
    "task": "Join the four tables so all 80 orders appear once, with customer_name, product_name, store_name, region and line_total (qty times unit_price, less the discount)."
  },
